This is the ultimate Memory Optimization pattern. While most patterns focus on making your code cleaner or more
flexible, the Flyweight pattern exists for one brutal reason: to prevent your application from crashing with a
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError.

The Problem: The RAM Explosion
Imagine you are building a video game like Minecraft, or a mapping application like Google Maps. You need to render a
massive forest with 1,000,000 trees.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer creates a simple Tree class.
Java
public class Tree {
    private int x;
    private int y;
    private String type;     // "Oak"
    private String color;    // "Green"
    private byte[] 3dModel;  // 1 Megabyte of 3D mesh data!
}

Why a Senior rejects this:
If you do new Tree() one million times, you are copying that 1 Megabyte 3dModel array one million times. Your game
will try to consume 1 Terabyte of RAM just for the trees, and the computer will instantly crash.

The Senior Solution: The Flyweight Pattern (Intrinsic vs Extrinsic)
A Senior Developer looks at that Tree class and realizes there are two completely different types of data inside it:

    1. Intrinsic State (Shared & Heavy): The type, color, and 3dModel. Every single "Oak" tree in the universe shares
    this exact same 1 Megabyte of data. It never changes.
    2. Extrinsic State (Unique & Light): The x and y coordinates. Every tree has a different location, but integers
    only take 4 bytes of RAM.

The Flyweight pattern says: "Rip the heavy, shared data out of the object. Store it in a cache exactly ONCE, and have
all 1,000,000 objects point to that exact same reference in memory."